Title:
CET-Compliant Call Stack Spoofing via Thread Pool + Enum Callback Trampolines + Indirect Syscalls

Type:
Blog Post (with GitHub PoC Tool)

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This write-up presents a Windows x64 call stack spoofing technique designed to survive modern EDR call-stack inspection and Intel CET shadow stack enforcement. It composes three primitives—thread pool execution (clean thread entry stack), enum-style callback trampolining (legitimate mid-stack frames from signed modules), and indirect syscalls (syscall return address inside ntdll)—so that every frame at syscall time is backed by Microsoft-signed code. The core contribution is a CET-compliant context switch that avoids `ret` (uses `jmp`) and reconciles the shadow stack pointer using `RDSSPQ`/`INCSSPQ`, preventing CET #CP faults without modifying unwind metadata. Implementation details cover manual unwinding with `RtlVirtualUnwind`, careful Win64 calling convention stack layout, and a covert per-thread handoff using `TEB.ArbitraryUserPointer`. The PoC is implemented in Rust with inline assembly and built with CET/CFG-related flags, and includes a catalog of 39 suitable enum/callback APIs in kernel32/kernelbase. It’s useful for red teamers and offensive researchers working on syscall/callstack evasion under CET, and for defenders building detections around anomalous call chains, CET instruction usage, and thread pool/enum callback behavior.

Technical Focus:
- Intel CET shadow stack mechanics and CET-compliant control-flow redirection (`RDSSPQ`/`INCSSPQ`, `jmp` vs `ret`)
- Call stack spoofing against kernel/EDR stack walking (unwind-based validation)
- Windows Thread Pool execution as a “clean stack base” primitive
- Enum callback trampolines to create legitimate signed mid-stack frames
- Indirect syscalls via `syscall; ret` gadgets in `ntdll.dll` and SSN handling
- Manual stack unwinding and frame reconstruction using `RtlVirtualUnwind` / `.pdata`

Use Cases:
- Execute sensitive NT syscalls (e.g., memory protection changes) with a fully module-backed call stack
- Research/validate CET-aware evasions and failure modes (shadow stack divergence, stale SSP entries)
- Build polymorphic call stacks by rotating among multiple enum/callback APIs
- Defensive engineering: develop detections for anomalous syscall call chains, thread pool work patterns, and CET instruction presence
- Study Win64 ABI edge cases when crafting stack frames and passing >4 arguments

Keywords:
Intel CET, shadow stack, RDSSPQ, INCSSPQ, #CP fault, call stack spoofing, indirect syscalls, syscall trampoline, ntdll, ZwProtectVirtualMemory, NtProtectVirtualMemory, Windows Thread Pool, CreateThreadpoolWork, SubmitThreadpoolWork, EnumSystemLocalesEx, enum callback, RtlVirtualUnwind, RtlCaptureContext, .pdata unwind info, TEB ArbitraryUserPointer, CFG, /CETCOMPAT, Rust inline assembly, EDR call stack inspection